// FILES IN PARALLEL AGAIN, AND SIX PLACES ARE WHY IT COULD NOT BE.
//
// This lane ran one file at a time from the outbox chapter, for a real reason: a
// PENDING migration lets two suites issue `CREATE TYPE` against one schema, and
// Postgres answers `duplicate key value violates unique constraint
// "pg_type_typname_nsp_index"` — an error about its own catalogue that reads like a
// driver fault. That window is one run wide, the first after a schema change, and a
// fresh database with every file racing is the probe that tests it: it passes.
//
// Serialising every file for ever to cover it cost 175 seconds a battery — this lane
// 176s to 102s and the gateway's 136s to 35s, measured either side. What actually kept
// the files apart was six places scoped wider than their subject, one per chapter:
//
//   the outbox chapter        a uniqueness claim over every ENVELOPE id in the table
//   the instruments chapter   bait on a subject no stream accepts, carrying no id
//   the presence chapter      a whole-table `outbox` count
//   the revisions chapter     two forged `connection.ack` samples, once the ack grew
//                             a required field
//   the notifications chapter two global drain totals read as one endpoint's
//   the limits chapter        two reads of a wall-clock minute bucket
//
// THE ESTIMATE SAID THREE, AND THE THREE IT NAMED WERE THE ONES ALREADY KNOWN —
// presence and the two limits reads, carried in from the features that found them.
// The other three came from RUNNING the lane unserialised, and two of those are not
// assertions at all: a fixture that plants unpublishable rows, and a sample frame
// invalidated by a required field added three chapters later. A count of what a fix
// drags with it is wrong in one direction, every time.
//
// Each is fixed where it lives. The migration race is left to the one run it can
// happen on.

